#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189241} :activity/id #uuid "78eb0962-178d-5a86-b546-15f3b9b19550" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051072 :topic/id #uuid "7eaea75e-b674-586e-bb97-853eab554ac2" :topic/math-academy-id 3866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188212 :question/id #uuid "b48c754c-18c0-575f-bc6a-54f6fbdcec62" :question/math-academy-id "e-9223"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050682}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188845}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "548270c0-0bd0-5fee-be88-b61420572e8a" :step/math-academy-id 34465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188005 :question/id #uuid "4af79cd3-8c50-533f-a744-3784a71a15dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10117"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188777}] :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"} :step/id #uuid "a7c34a66-a852-5efb-a910-810f804c9e9e" :step/math-academy-id 38155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189381 :tutorial/content "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF.\n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{(1 - e^{-x})(1 - e^{-2y}),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, y \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y)$, the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= lim_(x → ∞)[(1 - e^{-x})(1 - e^{-2y})] \\\\\n&= (1 - e^{-2y})lim_(x → ∞)(1 - \\frac{1}{e^{x}}) \\\\\n&= (1 - e^{-2y})lim_(x → ∞)(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n$$\n{1 - e^{-2y},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "86beb7b1-97a4-556a-840d-19cd1cf55cd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6304 :tutorial/title "Recovering a Marginal CDF"} :step/id #uuid "fceaea20-a10e-5bdb-9f66-378362b40cd6" :step/math-academy-id 38156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188066 :question/id #uuid "731d3a2d-4853-52e4-9c86-3654dc66adab" :question/math-academy-id "e-10436"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050502}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188776}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"} :step/id #uuid "bff20f81-9123-519a-9c01-c08964cb01aa" :step/math-academy-id 39347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189432 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X \\leq x,Y \\leq y)\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, we have the following relationship between the joint PDF and joint CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^y_{-\\infty}\\int^x_{-\\infty} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the joint CDF gives the probability that the random pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region $\\Delta$ that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\n![](images/ee/ee82c6b810f02f8b8380bd97c94cbe491095d17dbadc685ffdca1a54cf9828b8.png)\n\nThe joint CDF satisfies the following properties:\n\n- $0\\leq F(x,y)\\leq 1$\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty)=1$\n- $F(-\\infty,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$\n- The **marginal CDF** of $X$ is given by $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty)$.\n- The **marginal CDF** of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y)=F(\\infty,y)$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$F(x,y)=F_X(x)\\cdot F_Y(y)$.\n- The joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n$f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y)$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll explore the last four of these properties in more detail." :tutorial/id #uuid "d2e520b0-365b-5d48-9640-a2479b1c9a5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6981 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a3f7f2b-2869-5609-9787-9fdce0108444" :step/math-academy-id 42212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189119}}] :activity/title "Properties of the Joint CDF of Two Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188460}]}
